    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d554">

                <pb facs="1304" n="1190" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg1190"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-30">August 30,
                        1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for the Secretary of State</hi>
                </p>
                <p>I think you should talk this over with General Marshall.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d554-f1"/> Does it need a reply? My first reaction is that it is not
                    satisfactory in that it allows a “mere transit line” to go through Rome—and it
                    is of the highest military importance that this transit line be eliminated.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ranklin</hi>] D. R[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">oosevelt</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d554-f1" n="1" xml:id="d554-f1-content">
                <p>Roosevelt was referring to the memorandum of August 25, 1943, from the Apostolic
                    Delegation to the Department of State printed <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>,
                        <ref target="#pg1175">p. 1175</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
